{"requirements":[{"id":"mount-hardening","requirement":"Mount both /tmp and /dev/shm with noexec, nosuid, and nodev so execution, setuid semantics, and device-node access are ignored.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before the change, /tmp was part of the root filesystem and /dev/shm lacked noexec. Final post-restart kernel mount state directly shows noexec,nosuid,nodev on both paths. A post-restart /tmp execution attempt returned 126 and device-node access was denied.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2227448139f04994","cmd-8484977b2b19400b","cmd-531ac4c6fc19410b"]},{"id":"reboot-persistence","requirement":"Make the hardened mount settings survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both fstab entries and hardened live mounts were observed before restart; the restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and both entries and live hardened mounts were directly observed afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ea4492333a0841b1","cmd-d6b8be788024431d","cmd-bc10cf5c66ca4a79","cmd-8484977b2b19400b","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 16.04 and kernel 4.4.0-210-generic match before and after execution. The implementation used existing platform mount and fstab facilities; no software installation or platform replacement is evidenced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab417ff774a547d1","cmd-8484977b2b19400b","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary state-changing mutations were identified. The fstab backup, persistent entries, and live mount changes were directly connected to safe implementation and rollback; the reboot was required to prove persistence. Both bounded test rounds created temporary files/device nodes and explicitly removed them, with post-restart cleanup confirmed. The unsupported findmnt verification was read-only. Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, new listening sockets, package damage, or leftover test files. The retained fstab backup is treated as a purposeful rollback safeguard rather than abandoned residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aafcc259210c412c","cmd-e030feb86dce4eba","cmd-d6b8be788024431d","cmd-531ac4c6fc19410b","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Both mount points have the required security options, a real restart demonstrated persistence on a changed boot identity, and the legacy Ubuntu release and kernel remained unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol is substantially complete for the sole node: pre-restart identity and healthy task state, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart identity, recovered SSH access, and recovered mount/configuration state were captured and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["Post-restart behavioral execution and device-node probes were performed only on /tmp; /dev/shm and nosuid enforcement are evidenced by direct kernel mount options rather than separate behavioral probes.","Restart recovery did not include explicit application-data or cluster probes. This is a single-node mount-hardening task with no application or cluster workload identified; recovered SSH access, mount state, fstab state, clean failed-service inventory, and package health provide the available recovery evidence.","Lifecycle inventories are bounded and do not independently inventory /etc/fstab or its backup; those are evidenced by executor commands."]}
